#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163010} :activity/id #uuid "4a097ed8-748d-54f4-a06d-fefa2b69e0d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049206 :topic/id #uuid "e354e91d-75ea-5244-818d-ff4a2cb8189e" :topic/math-academy-id 3050} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163294 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two random variables with covariance $\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]$ and standard deviations $\\textrm {SD}[X]$ and $\\textrm {SD}[Y]$.\n\nThe **correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$,** also known as the **Pearson correlation coefficient**, is denoted $\\rho$ (the Greek letter \"rho\") and is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y]}{\\textrm {SD}[X]\\cdot \\textrm {SD}[Y]}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The correlation coefficient satisfies\n$-1\\leq \\rho \\leq 1$.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then\n$\\rho = 0$.\nThe reverse implication does not always hold. In other words, $\\rho=0$ does *not* imply that $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n- Unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ is a *dimensionless* quantity. This means we'll get the same answer regardless of the units used to measure $X$ and $Y$.\n- Also unlike the covariance, $\\rho$ tells the strength of the correlation between $X$ and $Y$. We typically have the following rule of thumb:If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\gt 0.7$, the correlation is **strong,** and the data generated by our two random variables closely follow a straight line.![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)If $0.3\\leq \\mid \\rho \\mid \\leq 0.7$, the correlation is **weak.** The data generated by our random variables follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)If $\\mid \\rho \\mid \\lt 0.3$, then the correlation is **negligible.** No (linear) correlation exists between the data points generated by the random variables.![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)\n\nLet's get some practice computing the correlation coefficient." :tutorial/id #uuid "96cc345f-656a-5faf-a2cf-47946504320d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5638 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5afd6705-a870-5fc5-8b7c-544c322a2815" :step/math-academy-id 34624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162140 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161706 :question/id #uuid "284f65cd-1288-56ed-80b7-f611b1464835" :question/math-academy-id "e-10372"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b371f15-7541-5cd9-ac1c-e53fd3b8fc18" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050351}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162441}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Correlation Coefficient Given Variances and Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "f053d13a-ebb8-5437-aa41-81a2459e4c6d" :step/math-academy-id 39086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162141 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161707 :question/id #uuid "d132664b-222c-5dd5-a52b-2471d10fcb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10373"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5fab7dc-3857-5840-a349-6d90e5f6e280" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162446}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correlation Coefficient of Two Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "ba6783eb-c401-5b3d-aa3b-85965b1f3255" :step/math-academy-id 39089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163123}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162142 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161708 :question/id #uuid "6562853a-696d-5cb5-a79d-76ff1a084d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-10384"} :knowledge-point/id #u